@meta
id: 17-01
title: Back to where you were
chapter: 17
type: lesson
skills: jump-back
par: 80
minutes: 5
vscode: Go to Definition, edit, Alt+Left
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Search for `assertCancellable`. The first match is the call inside
`cancel`, and that is the line you will end up changing. But not yet.

The helper it calls, at the bottom of the class, needs a second
parameter: `reason: string`. Go there first and add it. Then come back
to the call and pass `'CUSTOMER_REQUEST'` as the new argument.

In VS Code that is Go to Definition, type, Alt+Left. Vim has the same
idea built in: every time you jump somewhere, it remembers the line you
jumped *from*, and there is a two-key way back to it. Searching counts
as jumping. Pressing `j` forty times does not.

Both edits are graded.

@keys
| `''` | back to the line you were on before the last jump |
| `n` | next match of the search, which is itself a jump |
| `f)` | onto the closing paren, ready to insert before it |

@hints
Search once to land on the call, then step to the next match to reach
the definition. That second step is a jump, so vim has remembered the
call line for you.
--
Two apostrophes, `''`, jump to the line you were on before the latest
jump. It lands on the first non-blank character of that line.
--
`/assertCancellable` Enter, `n`, `f)`, insert `, reason: string`, Esc.
Then `''`, `f)`, insert `, 'CUSTOMER_REQUEST'`, Esc.

@solution
    /assertCancellable<Enter> n f)i, reason: string<Esc>
    '' f)i, 'CUSTOMER_REQUEST'<Esc> :wq<Enter>

Vim keeps a *jumplist*: a record of where the cursor was each time you
made a jump. A jump is any motion that can take you a long way in one
go: a search, `n` and `N`, `G` and `gg`, `%`, `{` and `}`, switching
buffers. Small motions like `j`, `w` and `f)` are not jumps and leave
no trace.

`''` goes to the line of the most recent entry. It is the same reflex
as Alt+Left, except it works after any jump, not only after Go to
Definition. Here `N` would have brought you back too, because you got
there with `n`. But if you had gone to the definition with `G` and a
few `k`, or with `%`, `N` would be useless and `''` would still work.

Two things to know about `''`. It only remembers one step: press it
twice and you bounce between the two positions. And it is linewise: it
lands on the first non-blank of the line, not on the column you left.
The next few lessons fix both.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { Order } from './order.entity';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    await this.customerProvider.findOne(customerId);

    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }

  public async create(args: { customerId: string; total: number }) {
    const customer = await this.customerProvider.findOne(args.customerId);

    const order = await this.orderRepository.create({
      values: { customerId: customer.id, total: args.total, status: 'PENDING' },
    });

    await this.emailService.send({ orderId: order.id, template: 'order_confirmation' });

    return order;
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PENDING');
    }

    await this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    this.assertCancellable(order);

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async refundPartial(id: string, amount: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (amount > order.total) {
      throw exception('REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { refundedAmount: amount },
    });
  }

  public assertCancellable(order: Order) {
    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { Order } from './order.entity';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    await this.customerProvider.findOne(customerId);

    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }

  public async create(args: { customerId: string; total: number }) {
    const customer = await this.customerProvider.findOne(args.customerId);

    const order = await this.orderRepository.create({
      values: { customerId: customer.id, total: args.total, status: 'PENDING' },
    });

    await this.emailService.send({ orderId: order.id, template: 'order_confirmation' });

    return order;
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PENDING');
    }

    await this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    this.assertCancellable(order, 'CUSTOMER_REQUEST');

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async refundPartial(id: string, amount: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (amount > order.total) {
      throw exception('REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { refundedAmount: amount },
    });
  }

  public assertCancellable(order: Order, reason: string) {
    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }
  }
}
